This is the direct brute-force version. It visits every subsequence, then checks whether the chosen values first rise and then fall. The change from rising to falling is allowed to happen at either end, so a fully increasing or fully decreasing subsequence is accepted too.

For `[1, 4, 3]`, the chosen subsequence `[1, 4, 3]` passes the check and gives length `3`. Repeated values fail because neither side is allowed to stay equal.

This is mainly useful for small inputs or for checking a faster solution. It does not change the input list.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    private int best;

    private bool IsBitonic(List<int> sequence) {
        if (sequence.Count == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        int index = 1;
        while (index < sequence.Count && sequence[index] > sequence[index - 1]) {
            index++;
        }

        while (index < sequence.Count && sequence[index] < sequence[index - 1]) {
            index++;
        }

        return index == sequence.Count;
    }

    private void Search(List<int> arr, int index, List<int> chosen) {
        if (index == arr.Count) {
            if (IsBitonic(chosen)) {
                best = Math.Max(best, chosen.Count);
            }
            return;
        }

        Search(arr, index + 1, chosen);

        chosen.Add(arr[index]);
        Search(arr, index + 1, chosen);
        chosen.RemoveAt(chosen.Count - 1);
    }

    public int LongestBitonicSequence(List<int> arr) {
        if (arr.Count == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        best = 1;
        Search(arr, 0, new List<int>());
        return best;
    }
}
```

There are `2^n` subsequences, and checking one can take `O(n)`, so the time complexity is `O(n * 2^n)`. The recursion and selected list use `O(n)` space, not counting the input.

I kept two lengths for every position. The first says how long an increasing subsequence can end there. The second says how long a decreasing subsequence can start there. If `i` is the peak, both parts can be joined by subtracting one because the peak was counted twice.

For example, with `[1, 4, 3, 2]`, position `1` has increasing length `2` and decreasing length `3`, so the answer there is `2 + 3 - 1 = 4`.

The input array is only read, so it is not changed. An empty array is also handled.

```cpp
class Solution {
  public:
    int LongestBitonicSequence(vector<int> arr) {
        int n = arr.size();
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> increasing(n, 1);
        vector<int> decreasing(n, 1);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[i]) {
                    increasing[i] = max(increasing[i], increasing[j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[i]) {
                    decreasing[i] = max(decreasing[i], decreasing[j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        int answer = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            answer = max(answer, increasing[i] + decreasing[i] - 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

Each index can be treated as the turning point. The first loop records increasing subsequences that finish at each index. The second loop walks backward and records decreasing subsequences that begin there. The largest combined value is the result.

Equal neighboring values are ignored in both comparisons. That keeps the rise and fall strict instead of allowing a flat part. With an array of length one, the initial length of one is returned.

```javascript
class Solution {
    LongestBitonicSequence(arr) {
        const n = arr.length;
        if (n === 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        const up = new Array(n).fill(1);
        const down = new Array(n).fill(1);

        for (let end = 0; end < n; end++) {
            for (let start = 0; start < end; start++) {
                if (arr[start] < arr[end]) {
                    up[end] = Math.max(up[end], up[start] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        for (let start = n - 1; start >= 0; start--) {
            for (let end = start + 1; end < n; end++) {
                if (arr[end] < arr[start]) {
                    down[start] = Math.max(down[start], down[end] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        let answer = 1;
        for (let peak = 0; peak < n; peak++) {
            answer = Math.max(answer, up[peak] + down[peak] - 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The array is read-only in this code. Time complexity is `O(n^2)`, while the two length arrays use `O(n)` extra space.

This version finds the two sides of every possible peak with recursion. `leftLength(i)` gives the best increasing subsequence that ends at `i`, while `rightLength(i)` gives the best decreasing subsequence that begins at `i`.

The arrays store already computed results, so the same index is not solved again. For a single element, both lengths are one, which gives the correct answer of one.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] values;
    private int[] increasingMemo;
    private int[] decreasingMemo;

    private int increasingLength(int index) {
        if (increasingMemo[index] != 0) {
            return increasingMemo[index];
        }

        int best = 1;
        for (int previous = 0; previous < index; previous++) {
            if (values[previous] < values[index]) {
                best = Math.max(best, increasingLength(previous) + 1);
            }
        }

        increasingMemo[index] = best;
        return best;
    }

    private int decreasingLength(int index) {
        if (decreasingMemo[index] != 0) {
            return decreasingMemo[index];
        }

        int best = 1;
        for (int next = index + 1; next < values.length; next++) {
            if (values[next] < values[index]) {
                best = Math.max(best, decreasingLength(next) + 1);
            }
        }

        decreasingMemo[index] = best;
        return best;
    }

    public int LongestBitonicSequence(int[] arr) {
        if (arr.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        values = arr;
        increasingMemo = new int[arr.length];
        decreasingMemo = new int[arr.length];

        int answer = 1;
        for (int peak = 0; peak < arr.length; peak++) {
            int left = increasingLength(peak);
            int right = decreasingLength(peak);
            answer = Math.max(answer, left + right - 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The two recursive tables together use `O(n)` space. The total time is `O(n^2)` because each index checks earlier or later positions.

## Intuition

A classic solution for Longest Bitonic Subsequence computes the LIS ending at every index and the LDS starting at every index in (O(n^2)). However, if the problem only asks for the maximum possible length and does not require reconstructing the actual subsequence, we can approach it differently.

Using the patience sorting technique, we can efficiently track the length of the longest increasing subsequence seen so far while scanning from left to right. Similarly, by scanning from right to left, we can track the length of the longest increasing subsequence in every suffix, which corresponds to the decreasing part of a bitonic sequence.

For every position, we treat it as a potential transition point between the increasing and decreasing phases. The best increasing contribution available up to that point is combined with the best decreasing contribution available after that point. Since the transition element is counted in both parts, we subtract one to avoid double counting.

This allows us to evaluate all possible transition points in (O(n \log n)) time.

## Approach

1. Traverse the array from left to right using the patience sorting LIS technique.

   * Maintain a temporary array `temp`.
   * `temp[len - 1]` stores the minimum possible tail value for an increasing subsequence of length `len`.
   * After processing index `i`, store the current LIS length in `dpInc[i]`.

2. Traverse the array from right to left using the same idea.

   * This captures the increasing subsequence lengths in suffixes, which correspond to the decreasing side of a bitonic sequence.
   * Store these values in `dpDec[i]`.

3. For every index `i`, combine the contributions:

   * Increasing part: `dpInc[i]`
   * Decreasing part: `dpDec[i]`
   * Total bitonic length: `dpInc[i] + dpDec[i] - 1`

4. The maximum value over all indices is the answer.

## Solution

```cpp
int LongestBitonicSequence(vector<int>& nums) {
    int n = nums.size();

    vector<int> dpInc(n, 1);
    vector<int> dpDec(n, 1);

    vector<int> temp;
    temp.push_back(nums[0]);

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if (nums[i] > temp.back()) {
            temp.push_back(nums[i]);
        } else {
            int ind = lower_bound(temp.begin(), temp.end(), nums[i]) - temp.begin();
            temp[ind] = nums[i];
        }

        dpInc[i] = temp.size();
    }

    temp.clear();
    temp.push_back(nums[n - 1]);

    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (nums[i] > temp.back()) {
            temp.push_back(nums[i]);
        } else {
            int ind = lower_bound(temp.begin(), temp.end(), nums[i]) - temp.begin();
            temp[ind] = nums[i];
        }

        dpDec[i] = temp.size();
    }

    int maxLen = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        maxLen = max(maxLen, dpInc[i] + dpDec[i] - 1);
    }

    return maxLen;
}
```

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:** **O(n*log n)**

Each element participates in a binary search within the patience sorting structure during both traversals.

**Space Complexity:** **O(n)**

The two auxiliary arrays and the temporary LIS container require linear extra space.

